Seasonal trends

Distribution of overall sampling effort


Code
site_sampling_effort &lt;- data |&gt;
  group_by(site_id) |&gt;
  summarise(sampling_effort = n()) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(sampling_effort))

ggplot(site_sampling_effort, aes(x = sampling_effort)) +
  geom_histogram()











Station most sampled
We see clear seasonal pattern with a clear peak of nitrites and nitrates after summer that decreases in autumn. We also notice that ammonium seems to peak just before, or long after nitrites and nitrates. This could be explained by the lifecycle of nitrogen compounds (check references).


Code
site_most_point &lt;- site_sampling_effort |&gt;
  slice(1) |&gt;
  pull(site_id) |&gt;
  first()

data_most_point &lt;- data |&gt;
  filter(site_id == site_most_point) |&gt;
  filter(lubridate::year(date) &gt; 2015)

ggplot(data_most_point, aes(x = date, y = value, color = parameter_code)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line()











Station sampled randomly
We sample random stations and plot their 2020 seasonal trend, to get an idea of the sampling effort during the year, per variable.


Code
data_2020 &lt;- data |&gt;
  filter(lubridate::year(date) == 2020)

selected_sites &lt;- data_2020 |&gt;
  pull(site_id) |&gt;
  unique() |&gt;
  sample(9)

selected_data &lt;- data_2020 |&gt;
  filter(site_id %in% selected_sites)

ggplot(selected_data, aes(x = date, y = value, color = parameter_code)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line() +
  facet_wrap(~site_id, scales = "free")









Code
ggplot(
  selected_data |&gt; filter(parameter_code %in% c("NH4", "PO4")),
  aes(x = date, y = value, color = parameter_code)
) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line() +
  facet_wrap(~site_id, scales = "free")









We can also have an idea of the average sampling effort per month per variable, to assess whether some nutrients are sampled during specific period each year, or not.


Code
sampling_per_month &lt;- data |&gt;
  mutate(month = as.factor(lubridate::month(date, label = TRUE))) |&gt;
  left_join(
    data |&gt;
      group_by(parameter_code) |&gt;
      summarise(n_tot = n(), .groups = "drop"),
    by = join_by(parameter_code)
  ) |&gt;
  group_by(parameter_code, month, n_tot) |&gt;
  summarise(sampling_effort = n(), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  mutate(
    rel_sampling_effort = sampling_effort / n_tot
  )

ggplot(
  sampling_per_month,
  aes(x = month, y = rel_sampling_effort, color = parameter_code, group = parameter_code)
) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line() +
  labs(color = "Parameter", x = "Month", y = "Average sampling effort")









The average sampling effort appears to be relatively even for nutrients.

Sampling methods

Overview over all stations
Raw yearly counts per method are dominated by the sampling effort trend above, which makes method changes hard to spot. We instead look at the share of observations per method each year, for each parameter.


Code
method_share &lt;- riversea::summarise_rephy_method_share(raw_data) |&gt;
  mutate(method = stringr::str_replace(
    method,
    "Spectrophotométrie",
    "Sp."
  )) |&gt;
  filter(year &gt;= 2000)

riversea::plot_rephy_method_share(
  method_share |&gt;
    filter(parameter_code == "NH4")
)









Code
riversea::plot_rephy_method_share(
  method_share |&gt;
    filter(parameter_code == "NO3+NO2")
)









Code
riversea::plot_rephy_method_share(
  method_share |&gt;
    filter(parameter_code == "OXYGENE")
)









Code
riversea::plot_rephy_method_share(
  method_share |&gt;
    filter(parameter_code == "PO4")
)









We notice a change in measurement methods for all parameters around 2006.


Focus on one station
We can try to see if this change of method is reflected in nutrient concentration by looking at a station where the change occurred.


Code
site &lt;- site_most_point

data_site &lt;- raw_data |&gt;
  filter(`Lieu de surveillance : Identifiant` == site)

method_share_site &lt;- riversea::summarise_rephy_method_share(data_site) |&gt;
  mutate(method = stringr::str_replace(
    method,
    "Spectrophotométrie",
    "Sp."
  )) |&gt;
  filter(year &gt;= 2000)

riversea::plot_rephy_method_share(method_share_site)









So for this station there is a clear change in sampling method. Let’s see if it is reflected in nutrient values.


Code
data_most_point &lt;- data |&gt;
  filter(site_id == site_most_point) |&gt;
  filter(lubridate::year(date) &gt;= 2003 & lubridate::year(date) &lt;= 2012)

ggplot(data_most_point, aes(x = date, y = value, color = parameter_code)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_line()









Luckily, we see that the change of methods doesn’t seem to cause a change in nutrient values.

3.2. Preliminary plots
Let’s plot abundance histogram for each species. As most species abundance are dominated by zeros, because of absence data, we plot log(abundance + 1) instead of raw abundances. This also implies that we will need to use zero-inflated distributions in our statistical model to account for this absence data.

catches |&gt;
    ggplot(aes(x = abundance + 1)) + # add 1 to avoid log(0)
    geom_histogram(binwidth = 0.5) +
    scale_x_log10() +
    facet_wrap(~species)








Let’s plot abundance time series for the 10 most abundant species.

n_species &lt;- 10
common_species &lt;- catches |&gt;
    group_by(species) |&gt;
    summarise(mean_abundance = mean(abundance)) |&gt;
    arrange(desc(mean_abundance)) |&gt;
    slice(1:n_species) |&gt;
    pull(species)

catches |&gt;
    filter(species %in% common_species) |&gt;
    group_by(year, species) |&gt;
    summarise(mean_abundance = mean(abundance)) |&gt;
    ggplot(aes(x = year, y = mean_abundance, colour = species)) +
    geom_line()








There is no clear trend, but we can’t say much at this point because trend may be masked by sampling efforts and environmental covariates.






Note



Here it would be interesting to plot the number of operations per year to see the evolution of the sampling effort.



d_eel &lt;- catches |&gt; filter(species == "ANG")
d_eel.avg &lt;- d_eel |&gt;
    group_by(pop_id) |&gt;
    summarise(abundance_mean = mean(abundance))
data &lt;- inner_join(d_elev.nn, d_eel.avg, by = "pop_id")

ggplot(data, aes(x = altitude, y = abundance_mean)) +
    geom_point() +
    labs(
        x = "Altitude (m)",
        y = "Eel abundance"
    )








We clearly observe the expected trend of eel abundance with altitude.
